  "docTitle": "Arrays and hashing - SDE Prep Dojo",
  "description": "Hash maps, sets, count arrays and canonical keys turn O(n^2) scans into one pass: two-sum, group anagrams, longest consecutive sequence, Kadane, cyclic sort and Boyer-Moore voting, with steppers and templates in Go, C++, Java and Python.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\">\n    <span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"2\">Level 2 - Beginner</span>\n    <span class=\"time\">45 min</span>\n    <span class=\"chip accent\">Pattern primer</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">SDE-1 core</span>\n  </div>\n  <h1>Arrays and hashing</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">Most \"easy\" and many \"medium\" array questions are one idea: stop re-scanning the array and remember what you have seen. A hash map remembers values, a count array remembers how many, a canonical key groups things that are \"the same\", and when you are not allowed extra memory the array itself becomes the hash table.</p>\n  <p class=\"small prereq\"><b>Before this page:</b> Big-O basics (<a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-complexity\">complexity</a>) and the map / set syntax of your language (<a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-language-toolkit\">language toolkit</a>). <b>Next:</b> <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-two-pointers\">Two pointers</a> and <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-sliding-window\">Sliding window</a>.</p>\n  <p class=\"small prereq\"><b>Go deeper:</b> <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-prefix-sum\">Prefix sums: subarray sum K, difference arrays, prefix XOR</a>, <a href=\"{{BASE}}coding/coding-range-query\">Range queries: Fenwick tree, segment tree, ordered map</a>.</p>\n"
  },
